---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import LinkRail from '../components/LinkRail.astro';

const path = '/trusted-partners/';

/* LOCAL REFERRALS — REBUILT FROM THE LEGACY SITE, 4 Sep 2026.
 *
 * WHERE THIS CAME BACK FROM, because it matters. The rebuild's redirect map
 * pointed /trusted-partners/ at /network/ on the assumption that it was
 * brand-network content. It was not. The legacy WordPress site is still live
 * at the production domain while this rebuild sits on a preview URL, so the
 * page could simply be fetched and read, and what it actually carried was a
 * referral list: one vetted local company, the services we do not offer, and
 * a stated bar for being listed. That is a genuinely useful page and it was
 * about to be thrown away on a guess. Read the old page before redirecting
 * it.
 *
 * THE REDIRECT IS GONE, NOT REPOINTED. `trusted-partners` was removed from
 * the legacy map in src/data/legacy-urls.json because this URL is now a real
 * page, and scripts/redirects.mjs fails the build if a redirect source
 * shadows a built page. Same URL as the legacy site, so nothing that links to
 * it from outside breaks.
 *
 * WHAT IS VERIFIED HERE AND WHAT IS NOT. Every detail about King of Kings
 * below was read off their own website on 4 Sep 2026 — the owner's name and
 * title, the phone number, and each service named — rather than copied from
 * our old page. Two of those had drifted in the legacy copy's phrasing. The
 * one claim that is ours rather than theirs is the recommendation itself, and
 * it is stated as ours.
 *
 * WHAT MUST NEVER BE ADDED HERE. A paid listing. The page states that nobody
 * pays to be on it, which turns every future addition into a promise being
 * kept or broken. If that ever changes, the sentence comes out first.
 *
 * THE BAR WAS RESTRUCTURED ON 8 SEP 2026, and the reason matters more than the
 * change. The original four conditions were written when the list held one
 * local window cleaner, and the second condition was "local — actually based
 * in and serving Whatcom or Skagit County". Coastal K9 fails that flatly: they
 * are in Aiken, South Carolina.
 *
 * The first pass therefore put them in a section of their own, outside the
 * bar. The owner then supplied the fact that changes the analysis: this is not
 * a referral at all. Sasquatch holds the account and CONTRACTS THEM IN for
 * detection and mass removal on multifamily, commercial and large food-plant
 * work. That is a subcontract, and a subcontract is a different relationship
 * from handing somebody a phone number and stepping back.
 *
 * So the page now carries TWO bars rather than one weakened one. That
 * distinction is the honest way to do this and it is worth defending:
 *
 *   LOCAL REFERRALS — work we do not do at all, handed over completely, we are
 *   out of it once the introduction is made. Local matters enormously here,
 *   because the customer's entire recourse becomes that company.
 *
 *   SPECIALISTS WE BRING IN — a capability that does not exist in this county,
 *   engaged on a specific job while WE stay accountable for the account.
 *   Locality is not the relevant test; capability, certification and who
 *   carries the responsibility are.
 *
 * WHAT WAS NOT DONE: the local bar was not loosened to admit somebody who does
 * not meet it. It still says local, it still says licensed and insured in
 * Washington, and King of Kings still has to clear all four. Widening a
 * standard to fit a case you have already decided on is how a stated standard
 * becomes decoration, and this page's only value is that its standard is real.
 *
 * ON LICENSING, and the wording is careful. Coastal K9 is not licensed in
 * Washington and the page says so. What it does NOT do is opine on what their
 * regulatory position permits — that is not ours to assert. It states our own
 * conduct instead: anything applied in Washington is applied by us, under our
 * licenses. That sentence is unimpeachable because it is about us.
 *
 * JORGE IS NOT A REFERRAL EITHER and is filed separately for the same kind of
 * reason. He consults TO this company; he is not somebody we send customers
 * to. See OWNER DECISION #3 in business.ts — naming him is permitted,
 * describing him as staff is not, and the section here repeats neither the
 * wording nor the claims of the fuller treatment on /about/.
 *
 * WHY IT IS FILED AS A UTILITY PAGE in scripts/harness.mjs. The M1 content
 * floor is 3,000 words and this page cannot honestly reach it: it exists to
 * hand a reader somebody else's phone number and get out of the way, and
 * padding a referral to three thousand words would make it worse at the only
 * job it has. Same class as /contact/ and /network/, and declared in the open
 * in the harness rather than applied quietly.
 */

/* 40–60 words on the answer (src/lib/seo.ts checkAnswer) — recount on edit.
   This is 52. */
const answer =
  'Two different things are on this page. Local companies we hand work to when it falls outside pest control, and specialists we contract in when a job needs a capability this county does not have. Nobody pays to be listed on either, and the standards for each are set out here.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do companies pay to be listed on this page?',
    a: 'No. There is no fee, no commission and no referral arrangement. The list is short for exactly that reason — it is made up of businesses we already call when a job falls outside pest control, not businesses that asked to be here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Sasquatch Pest Control do window or gutter cleaning?',
    a: 'No. We handle the pests that turn up in gutters, on glass and under eaves — rodents using a debris-packed gutter as a route to the roofline, wasp nests in soffits, spider webbing across window frames — but the cleaning itself is a different trade with different equipment and different insurance. We refer that work out.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I check a company you refer me to?',
    a: 'The same way you should check us. Washington State publishes a free contractor and business verification tool through the Department of Labor & Industries that shows registration status, workers\' compensation coverage and any citations. It takes a couple of minutes and it is worth doing before anybody works on your property.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I get my business listed here?',
    a: 'Get in touch, but read the bar first. A company has to be licensed and insured for the work it does in Washington, actually based in and serving Whatcom or Skagit County, offering something we do not offer rather than competing with us, and a business we would hire at our own homes. Most inquiries fail the last one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens if a listed company stops meeting that standard?',
    a: 'They come off the page. That is the whole reason this list is short and the reason it is worth anything — a referral we would not stand behind is worse for us than no referral at all, because the customer heard the recommendation from us.',
  },
];

const rails = [
  { href: '/services/', label: 'Everything we do handle' },
  { href: '/services/exclusion-and-repairs/', label: 'Pest exclusion & repairs' },
  { href: '/awards/', label: 'Awards, press and how to verify a company' },
  { href: '/about/', label: 'Our licenses and our crew' },
  { href: '/contact/', label: 'Get in touch' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  path={path}
  title="Trusted Partners & Local Referrals | Sasquatch Pest"
  description="The local companies we send customers to for work Sasquatch Pest Control does not do. Nobody pays to be listed, and the bar for getting on the page is set out here."
  kind="page"
  faqs={faqs}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Trusted partners', path },
  ]}
>
  <h1>Local referrals: who we send people to</h1>
  <AnswerBox text={answer} />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      There are jobs we do not do. When a customer asks about one, we would
      rather send them to somebody good than shrug.
    </p>
    <p>
      The companies on this page are local, licensed and insured, and they do
      work we would be comfortable having done at our own homes.{' '}
      <b>Nobody pays to be listed here.</b> There is no fee, no commission and
      no arrangement of any kind — these are simply the businesses we call when
      a job falls outside what Sasquatch Pest Control does. If a company on
      this page ever stops meeting that standard, it comes off.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is why the local list is one name long rather than twenty. A page of
      twenty referrals is a directory, and a directory nobody vetted is worth
      nothing to the person reading it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Further down are two things that are not referrals at all. One is a
      canine detection company we contract in on jobs where a dog will do
      something no person can — we keep the account and stay responsible for
      it. The other is the entomologist this company consults. Neither is
      somebody we hand you off to, so neither belongs in the list above, and
      each is labeled for what it actually is.
    </p>

    <h2>King of Kings Window Cleaning</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Exterior cleaning and window care · Bellingham, WA · serving Whatcom
      and Skagit County</b>
    </p>
    <p>
      King of Kings Window Cleaning is a locally owned exterior cleaning
      company based in Bellingham, founded and run by Randy Fee. Like us, it is
      run from here rather than dispatched from a franchise desk in another
      state.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>What they handle:</b> residential and commercial window cleaning,
      gutter cleaning and downspout clearing, roof cleaning, moss removal,
      pressure washing, house washing and solar panel cleaning.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Why we send people to them.</b> Pest work and exterior cleaning run
      into each other constantly, and the overlap is not incidental. Spider
      webbing and droppings across glass and window frames. Wasp nests tucked
      up under eaves and soffits. Gutters packed with needles and leaf litter,
      which hold moisture against the fascia and give rodents a covered highway
      to the roofline — one of the most common contributing conditions we find
      on a rodent job in this county, and one we can point at but not fix.
    </p>
    <p>
      We handle the pests. The cleanup, and the conditions that keep inviting
      them back, are a different trade. King of Kings does that work properly.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.kingofkingswindowcleaning.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">
        kingofkingswindowcleaning.com
      </a>{' '}
      · <a href="tel:+13603036806">(360) 303-6806</a>
    </p>

    <h2>Canine detection: Coastal K9 &amp; Pest Solutions</h2>
    <p>
      <b>K9 bed bug and rodent detection · Aiken, South Carolina · contracted
      in for detection and mass removal</b>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a referral and we are not handing you off. Where a job needs
      a detection dog, we bring Coastal K9 in on it. The account stays with us,
      the responsibility stays with us, and anything applied in Washington is
      applied by us under our own licenses.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are based in Aiken, South Carolina, which is exactly why they sit
      here rather than in the list above — that list is for local companies we
      hand work to entirely, and locality matters there because the customer's
      whole recourse becomes that company. This is a different arrangement with
      a different test, and the test is capability.
    </p>

    <h3>What they are brought in for</h3>
    <p>
      <b>K9 bed bug detection in multifamily and commercial buildings.</b> This
      is where a dog earns its keep and a person cannot compete. Sweeping forty
      apartments, a hotel floor or a care facility by eye is slow, and
      low-level activity is exactly what visual inspection misses — a handful
      of insects in one seam of one bed frame in one unit. A canine sweep
      narrows the work to the rooms that actually have something, which means
      the other thirty-odd units are left alone rather than treated on
      suspicion.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Rodent detection in large commercial facilities that want to avoid
      chemicals.</b> Food processing, cold storage, agricultural plants and
      similar sites frequently have a policy reason or a customer-audit reason
      to keep pesticide use to a minimum, and some want none at all in
      production areas. Detection plus physical removal is the route to that.
      A dog finds harborage inside structures where a visual inspection cannot
      reach, and what follows is trapping, exclusion and removal rather than
      anything applied.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Mass removal.</b> We run maintenance service — the routes, the
      monitoring, the ongoing work — and that is a different job from clearing
      out a site that has got badly away from somebody. Where the scale of a
      removal exceeds what a maintenance crew should be attempting, they can be
      contracted in for it.
    </p>

    <h3>Who they are, and what is checkable</h3>
    <p>
      Coastal K9 is run by Kimberly Camera, a FEMA-trained urban search and
      rescue handler, and the teams carry NESDCA certification — the National
      Entomology Scent Detection Canine Association, which is the body that
      certifies bed bug detection dogs. Alongside the canine work they run
      nationwide rodent detection and mitigation aimed at food-processing and
      agricultural facilities.
    </p>
    <p>
      All of that is read off their own materials rather than watched by us,
      and it is worth saying which parts we can and cannot stand behind. They
      are not licensed in Washington, which is why applications here are ours.
      What we can speak to is the arrangement: when we bring somebody onto a
      site under our name, the result is our problem, and that is the whole
      reason this section exists rather than a phone number.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://ck9ps.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">ck9ps.com</a>
    </p>

    <h3>What a detection dog is actually doing</h3>
    <p>
      Worth understanding before commissioning one, because the sales version
      of this is more impressive than the real one and the real one is still
      good.
    </p>
    <p>
      The dog is not finding bed bugs the way a person finds them, by seeing
      one. It is trained on the scent of live insects and viable eggs, and it
      indicates where that scent is strongest. Two consequences follow. It can
      find activity a person would walk past — a small number of insects deep
      in a seam, a frame joint, a wall void — which is the entire point. And it
      is telling you about odor rather than showing you an insect, which is why
      a finding is confirmed visually wherever confirmation is possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      A dog is a screening tool, not a laboratory instrument. Performance in a
      real building depends on the training, the handler, how the search is
      run, and how tired the animal is on the fourth hour of the third floor.
      That is precisely why third-party certification matters more here than in
      most trades: anybody can call a dog a bed bug dog, and NESDCA is an
      outside body testing against a standard rather than a company vouching
      for itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ask any canine provider — including one we bring in — who certified the
      team, when, and whether the handler will show you the certificate. A
      provider who is uncomfortable with that question has answered it.
    </p>

    <h3>Scoping a sweep on a multifamily building</h3>
    <p>
      The commonest mistake a property manager makes is commissioning the wrong
      shape of job, so here is how the decision usually goes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Targeted or whole-building.</b> If one unit has reported and the
      building is small, a sweep of that unit plus the ones sharing walls,
      floor and ceiling with it is usually the right scope — bed bugs move
      along shared structure, so the neighbors are the question, not the whole
      block. If reports have come from units that are not adjacent, that
      pattern says the population has already spread and a whole-building sweep
      is the cheaper answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Preparation matters more than people expect.</b> Clutter reduces what
      any search can achieve, and strong competing odors — fresh paint,
      cleaning chemicals, air fresheners plugged in at the wall — make a dog's
      job harder. Rooms should be accessible and reasonably ordinary. Nobody
      needs to empty an apartment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Notice and access are the real constraint.</b> A sweep needs entry to
      every unit in scope on the same day, and in tenanted housing that means
      proper notice in the form your state and your leases require. A sweep
      that reaches sixty percent of the units in scope has not answered the
      question it was commissioned to answer, and the gaps are exactly where
      the next report comes from.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>What you should get at the end.</b> A unit-by-unit record of where the
      dog indicated and where it did not, with visual confirmation where it was
      possible to obtain. That document is what turns a sweep into a treatment
      plan and a defensible record — and for a manager dealing with residents,
      the units that came back clear are worth as much as the ones that did
      not.
    </p>

    <h3>Chemical-free is a real request, and a specific one</h3>
    <p>
      A growing number of commercial sites ask for pest control with minimal or
      no pesticide use, and the reasons are usually concrete rather than
      ideological: a customer audit standard, a certification scheme, an
      organic line, a facility where product is exposed, or a residential
      population where somebody is chemically sensitive.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is worth separating what is achievable from what is not. Detection,
      exclusion, trapping, sanitation and physical removal involve no
      pesticide, and between them they do more of the durable work than
      chemistry does — this company argues that on every other page of this
      site. A great deal of rodent work in particular can be done without
      applying anything at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      What we will not do is promise that any site can be run indefinitely with
      nothing applied under any circumstances. That depends on the building,
      the pressure and what happens next, and a company that promises it in
      advance is making a claim it cannot control. What we will do is tell you
      before we apply anything, why, and what the alternative would have cost
      in time or effectiveness.
    </p>

    <h3>When a dog is not the answer</h3>
    <p>
      Worth saying, because detection is expensive and it is easy to sell.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a single-family house with a confirmed, visible bed bug problem, a
      dog tells you what you already know. For one apartment in a small
      building, an inspection and a monitor will usually settle it. For a
      rodent problem where the runs, the droppings and the damage are plainly
      visible in a crawlspace, the finding is already made.
    </p>
    <p>
      Detection is worth paying for when the question is genuinely open and the
      cost of guessing is high — which units, is it still active, where inside
      a structure, and how much of a building has to be treated. That is a
      multifamily and commercial question far more often than a residential
      one, and if you ask us for a dog on a job that does not need one we will
      tell you so.
    </p>

    <h2>The entomologist we consult</h2>
    <p>
      <b>Jorge Bedoya, Associate Certified Entomologist</b>
    </p>
    <p>
      This one is the other way round from everything else on the page. Jorge
      is not somebody we send you to — he is somebody we go to. He holds the
      Associate Certified Entomologist credential from the Entomological
      Society of America and reviews species identification and treatment
      protocols for this company.
    </p>
    <p>
      He is a consultant rather than a member of our staff, which means he does
      not run routes, attend jobs or supervise technicians. And ACE is a
      professional certification awarded by a scientific society, not a
      Washington pesticide license — a different credential doing a different
      job from the WSDA licenses our technicians hold.
    </p>
    <p>
      He is named here because knowing who a company asks when it is not sure
      is worth as much as knowing who turns up at the door. The fuller account,
      including what the arrangement is actually for, is on our{' '}
      <a href="/about/">about page</a> alongside the license numbers.
    </p>

    <h2>Services we do not provide</h2>
    <p>
      So there is no confusion about where our work stops, here is what
      Sasquatch Pest Control does not do — and who we would point you to
      instead.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Window cleaning, gutter cleaning, roof cleaning and moss removal —
        King of Kings Window Cleaning
      </li>
      <li>
        Pressure washing, house washing and solar panel cleaning — King of
        Kings Window Cleaning
      </li>
      <li>
        Canine bed bug or rodent detection — we have no detection dog and no
        handler, and nobody in this county does. See Coastal K9 above.
      </li>
      <li>
        Wood-destroying organism reports for a real estate transaction — that
        requires a Washington structural pest inspector license, which is a
        separate credential from the pesticide applicator licenses we hold. Ask
        your lender or agent for an inspector; we can treat what one finds.
      </li>
      <li>
        Structural repair beyond exclusion work — we seal, screen, flash and
        replace the materials rodents and insects come through, but framing,
        roofing and siding replacement are a contractor's job.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For everything we do handle, see our{' '}
      <a href="/services/">pest control services</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this page exists at all</h2>
    <p>
      Most pest control companies do not publish anything like this, and the
      reason is straightforward: every line on it is a line about work we are
      not taking.
    </p>
    <p>
      Naming a window cleaner sends a job away. Explaining when a detection dog
      is unnecessary talks somebody out of the most expensive thing on offer.
      Setting out a standard in public means it can be held against us the
      first time somebody appears here who does not meet it. None of that
      generates an invoice.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is here because of what happens on the other side of it. A customer
      who asks about gutters and gets a straight answer and a good number
      remembers that, and they call about the wasps in August. A property
      manager who is told a dog would not tell them anything they do not
      already know believes the next thing we say about a job that does need
      one. The trade this page makes is a small amount of work now against
      being the company somebody trusts later, and we would take that trade
      every time.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also does something quieter. Writing down where our work stops forces
      us to be clear about it internally, and a company that is vague about its
      own boundaries ends up taking jobs it should not, which is how people get
      let down.
    </p>

    <h2>Check anybody before you hire them, including us</h2>
    <p>
      A referral from us is a starting point, not a substitute for looking
      something up. Washington State publishes a free verification tool through
      the Department of Labor &amp; Industries covering business registration,
      workers' compensation coverage and citation history — it needs no account
      and takes about two minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Our <a href="/awards/">awards and recognition page</a> sets out how to
      use that tool and the Department of Agriculture's license search, with
      our own numbers to try them on. Run them on us first if you like. That is
      rather the point of publishing them.
    </p>

    <h2>The two standards on this page</h2>
    <p>
      There are two different relationships here and they are held to two
      different standards. Running them together would let either one borrow
      whatever the other had earned, so they are set out separately.
    </p>

    <h3>To be listed as a local referral</h3>
    <p>
      This is work we do not do at all, handed over completely. Once the
      introduction is made we are out of it, and the customer's entire recourse
      becomes that company — which is why the bar is what it is. A business
      needs to be:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Licensed and insured for the work they do in Washington State</li>
      <li>
        Local — actually based in and serving Whatcom or Skagit County, not a
        national call center routing leads
      </li>
      <li>
        Complementary, not competing — a service Sasquatch Pest Control does
        not offer
      </li>
      <li>
        Somebody we would hire ourselves, with work and customer care we are
        willing to put our name behind
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That standard has not moved to fit anybody, and it is not going to. A bar
      that widens whenever a case does not clear it is decoration.
    </p>

    <h3>To be contracted in as a specialist</h3>
    <p>
      This is a capability that does not exist in this county, brought onto a
      job we are already running. We keep the account, we stay accountable for
      the outcome, and anything applied in Washington is applied under our
      licenses. Locality is not the test here, because the customer is not
      being handed anywhere. What matters is:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A capability nobody in Whatcom or Skagit County actually offers — not a
        cheaper version of something available locally
      </li>
      <li>
        Certification from a recognized body for the specific capability,
        named, so it can be looked up rather than taken on trust
      </li>
      <li>
        Complementary, not competing — brought in on our job, not introduced to
        our customer
      </li>
      <li>
        Work we are willing to have carried out under our name, because that is
        exactly what is happening
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Think your business fits either? <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>.
      Nobody pays to be on this page and nobody ever will.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Faq items={faqs} />

  <LinkRail heading="Related" links={rails} />

  <Cta market="Whatcom County" />
</BaseLayout>
